  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=x{y}^{2}-1,\\,y(-1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method with step size $\u0394x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Fill in the empty box in the column for $\u0394y$ in the table below using this method. Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,\u0394y\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |",
  "worked_solution": "First, because the initial condition is $y(-1)=2,$ we fill $y=2$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,\u0394y\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{\u2032}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =x{y}^{2}-1 \\\\  & =(-1)(2)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,\u0394y\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $\u0394y$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0394y & ={y}^{\u2032}\\cdot \u0394x \\\\  & =-5(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,\u0394y\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ |",
